{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '36,150p'\"","aggregated_output":"    36\t\\section{Introduction}\n    37\tPairwise judgments offer a common interface for comparing systems even when an absolute quality scale is difficult to define. A user can prefer one response to another; a game supplies a winner; a statistical model turns the resulting comparison graph into scores and ranks. The Bradley--Terry construction is a familiar example \\citep{Hunter2004}. Human-preference platforms such as Chatbot Arena make these rankings particularly consequential for model comparison \\citep{Chiang2024}. Their interpretation nevertheless requires separating several questions: how uncertain is the ordering under resampling, how well does the model describe a population, and how much can the fitted ordering change when selected observations change?\n    38\t\n    39\tThis paper addresses the last question. We hold the observed matchups, participant set, estimator, and regularization fixed, and change only selected binary outcomes. The distinction matters: an intervention can exploit a narrow fitted score gap without demonstrating that either participant has changed in underlying quality. Conversely, an uncertain rank need not imply that arbitrary small edits will have much effect. A finite-change stress test complements rather than replaces sampling-based uncertainty.\n    40\t\n    41\tOur research question is whether guided outcome changes produce more ordering disruption than simple matched-budget interventions, and whether the strength of that comparison is consistent across the observed panels. The empirical setting includes a crowdsourced preference panel and sports-season panels. This spans different data-generating designs, but it is not a controlled experiment on design: the graphs, participant counts, tie frequencies, and outcome mechanisms differ. We therefore report case-specific evidence before an aggregate summary.\n    42\t\n    43\tThe method uses local influence calculations to screen feasible edits, but evaluates candidate rankings through nonlinear refits. A bounded path search and optional exchanges coordinate nested edit sets across budgets. The scientific contribution is the resulting finite-change comparison, not an isolated attribution to exchange or a claim of globally optimal manipulation. Three controls distinguish directed selection from uniform changes, approximate exposure matching, and selection of close observed matchups. Conditional grouped resampling makes the strength and limits of the comparisons visible.\n    44\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L194-L475]]\n    45\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task_contract.json#/method]]\n    46\t\n    47\tThe evidence supports a deliberately narrow conclusion. At the largest nominal budget, the guided Arena intervention disrupts 11.36\\% of orderings; all recorded Arena paired intervals favor it over each comparator. Several NBA comparisons remain inconclusive despite larger guided point estimates. We contribute an explicit fixed-estimator stress-test definition, a reproducible comparison across these panels, and an interpretation that keeps attainable disruption separate from optimality, statistical certainty, and population generalization.\n    48\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    49\t\n    50\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    51\t\\centering\n    52\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/sensitivity_overview.png}\n    53\t\\caption{Finite-flip sensitivity study. Observed matchups and the estimator remain fixed while nested binary-outcome edits are selected and exactly refitted. The guided intervention is compared with matched-budget controls; paired grouped resampling conditions on the selected intervention plans. Arena and NBA are separate, unequal evidence blocks, not a causal comparison of evaluation designs.}\n    54\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    55\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L194-L475]]\n    56\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task_contract.json#/method]]\n    57\t\\end{figure}\n    58\t\n    59\t\\section{Related Work}\n    60\t\\paragraph{Models and ranking targets.}\n    61\tStatistical comparison models connect qualitative judgments to latent scales \\citep{Thurstone1927}; tournament-strength estimation also has a long likelihood-based history \\citep{Zermelo1929}. Bradley--Terry fitting and its generalizations have established computational treatments \\citep{Hunter2004,Turner2012}; spectral inference offers another computational perspective on choice models \\citep{Maystre2015}. Explicit tie models introduce additional structure \\citep{Rao1967,Davidson1970}; our fractional-outcome convention instead preserves the fixed estimator under study. Separation can complicate unpenalized logistic estimation \\citep{Albert1984}; our fixed quadratic penalty is distinct from bias-reduced likelihood estimation \\citep{Firth1993}.\n    62\t\n    63\tAlternative ranking formulations emphasize graph structure and different estimands. Rank Centrality uses a random-walk interpretation \\citep{Negahban2017}, while combinatorial Hodge theory distinguishes globally consistent rankings from cyclic components \\citep{Jiang2011}. We do not compare estimators here. We measure changed full-order relations and report normalized rank displacement as a secondary quantity, related to permutation disarray \\citep{Diaconis1977}. Full-order disruption should not be confused with a top-list comparison \\citep{Fagin2003} or with a change in the identity of the best participant.\n    64\t\n    65\t\\paragraph{Influence and uncertainty.}\n    66\tInfluence-function analysis studies local sensitivity \\citep{Hampel1974}; influential-observation diagnostics and model-level explanations develop related perspectives \\citep{Cook1977,Koh2017}. Finite-sample robustness analysis asks whether removing a small subset changes a conclusion \\citep{Broderick2020}. Here the allowed intervention is an outcome flip rather than removal, and finite edits are assessed through refitting. We make no comprehensive novelty claim about ranking sensitivity.\n    67\t\n    68\tBootstrap methodology provides a separate route to assessing variability \\citep{Efron1979,Efron1986,Davison1997}. Dependence within observations motivates grouped resampling rather than treating every record as independent \\citep{Cameron2008}. Our percentile intervals are conditional diagnostics, not a claim of the bootstrap refinements studied in that literature. More generally, robust estimation distinguishes an ideal model from departures from it \\citep{Huber1964}. The stress tests below concern the observed fitted rankings, without assuming that the model captures all preference heterogeneity or sports dynamics.\n    69\t\n    70\t\\section{Method}\n    71\t\\subsection{Problem Setup and Estimand}\n    72\tLet $D=\\{(a_r,b_r,y_r,z_r)\\}_{r=1}^n$ contain comparisons between $p$ participants. Outcomes are $y_r\\in\\{0,\\tfrac12,1\\}$; $z_r$ is an optional home covariate. With $x_r=e_{a_r}-e_{b_r}$, augmented by $z_r$ when a home coefficient is fitted, the estimator minimizes\n    73\t\\begin{equation}\n    74\t L_D(\\theta)=\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\left[\\log(1+\\exp(x_r^\\top\\theta))-y_rx_r^\\top\\theta\\right]\n    75\t       +\\frac12\\|\\theta\\|_2^2 .\n    76\t \\label{eq:objective}\n    77\t\\end{equation}\n    78\tParticipant scores are centered. The NBA home coefficient, when present, receives the same penalty. This is a sum-loss objective: neither sample-size normalization nor regularization strength changes after an edit. The fixed penalty makes the objective strictly convex; the implementation uses converged numerical solutions rather than an exact-arithmetic claim. Standard convex-optimization distinctions between an objective and its numerical solution remain relevant \\citep{Bubeck2015}.\n    79\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L61-L172]]\n    80\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task_contract.json#/method]]\n    81\t\n    82\tThe admissible intervention is an ordered list $\\pi$ of distinct observed rows with binary outcomes. Its prefix $S_k$ replaces $y_r$ by $1-y_r$ for $r\\in S_k$. Ties contribute half outcomes to every fit but are not editable. Matchups, participant identities, multiplicities, and home indicators remain unchanged. Nominal fractions $q\\in\\{.001,.005,.01\\}$ give integer costs $k_q=\\lfloor qn\\rfloor$, where $n$ includes ties. Thus the denominator is not the number of editable rows; actual costs must accompany nominal percentages. All prefixes are nested, precluding independent selection of unrelated sets at different budgets.\n    83\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task_contract.json#/method]]\n    84\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    85\t\n    86\tLet $\\rho_0$ and $\\rho_k$ be the total orderings of fitted participant scores before and after editing, with source-index tie breaking. The primary endpoint is\n    87\t\\begin{equation}\n    88\t D_k=\\binom{p}{2}^{-1}\\sum_{i<j}\n    89\t \\mathbf{1}\\!\\left\\{\\operatorname{ord}_{\\rho_0}(i,j)\\ne\n    90\t                         \\operatorname{ord}_{\\rho_k}(i,j)\\right\\}.\n    91\t \\label{eq:disruption}\n    92\t\\end{equation}\n    93\tIt is a fraction of participant-pair orderings, not a fraction of changed votes, misclassified observations, or displaced top positions. Its companion is normalized mean rank displacement, $\\sum_i|\\rho_k(i)-\\rho_0(i)|/[p(p-1)]$. A feasible intervention establishes attainable disruption. It does not establish the smallest sufficient edit set, the largest possible disruption, or robustness against all other interventions.\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task_contract.json#/metrics]]\n    95\t\n    96\t\\subsection{Design Rationale: Screen Locally, Assess Finite Changes}\n    97\tThe Hessian at the unedited solution is\n    98\t\\begin{equation}\n    99\t H=\\sum_r\\sigma(x_r^\\top\\hat\\theta)\n   100\t       [1-\\sigma(x_r^\\top\\hat\\theta)]x_rx_r^\\top+I.\n   101\t\\end{equation}\n   102\tFor a unit outcome change, implicit differentiation gives $\\partial\\hat\\theta/\\partial y_r=H^{-1}x_r$. A binary flip therefore has first-order shift $(1-2y_r)H^{-1}x_r$. The frozen screening contrast is the narrowest adjacent score gap among the highest-ranked participants in the prescribed top subset. Its signed row influence is the projection of this shift onto the contrast. The available implementation chooses that subset as the top $\\min(10,p)$ participants.\n   103\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L34-L44]]\n   104\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L510-L542]]\n   105\t\n   106\tThe rationale is that local directions can identify promising ways to cross close score gaps, but a finite intervention changes the fit and may alter which gaps matter. Influence is therefore a proposal mechanism, not the reported finite-change ranking. This distinction also avoids interpreting agreement with derivative arithmetic as evidence that a linear approximation remains accurate at the largest budget.\n   107\t\n   108\t\\subsection{Guided Refit and Complete-Path Selection}\n   109\tThe supplied selection implementation combines a fixed influence ordering with a width-two exact-refitted path search. At each budget, proposals use the frozen contrast, its current local analogue, and up to six currently close baseline-oriented participant contrasts. Equal design rows are aggregated into sufficient statistics for fitting, while flip capacity and returned indices refer to original observations. Aggregation does not remove repeated observations or change their weight.\n   110\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L61-L80]]\n   111\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L194-L266]]\n   112\t\n   113\tPaths are compared lexicographically by sums across requested budgets of absolute rank displacement, inversion count, and a smooth inversion potential. Thus displacement is the leading internal objective; the reported $D_k$ is not its exact optimization target. The fixed influence path is retained as a complete alternative, and the strongest complete path under the internal criterion is passed to optional refinement.\n   114\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L211-L266]]\n   115\t\n   116\tRefinement considers swaps of selected positions and replacements by unused binary rows. Only affected prefixes require rescoring: a swap at zero-based positions $u<v$ changes budgets $u<k\\le v$, while replacement at position $u$ changes budgets $k>u$. Cached exact-refit results are keyed by the sorted original-row prefix. Screening uses the current smooth-potential gradient and the original inverse Hessian; a change is accepted only when its complete lexicographic score strictly improves. A bounded search retains the complete incumbent if refinement is exhausted or fails. This is heuristic selection, not exhaustive optimization. The code supports these operations, but no accepted-swap trace or component-isolating final comparison is available; the results cannot identify which operation explains an advantage.\n   117\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L309-L475]]\n   118\t\n   119\t\\subsection{Matched-Budget Comparison Families}\n   120\t\\textbf{Uniform} selects binary rows without directed influence screening. \\textbf{Degree-stratified} matches the guided prefix's counts within quartile bins of the sum of the two participants' comparison degrees. This approximates exposure matching; it does not fix individual endpoint counts, participant identity, or outcome direction. \\textbf{Small margin} selects rows with the smallest absolute fitted matchup margin. A close matchup is not necessarily a close adjacent rank contrast, so this control tests a different local-selection rule.\n   121\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task_contract.json#/method]]\n   122\t\n   123\tAll methods use the same observed input, estimator, legal edit units, and integer budgets. The random controls average their fixed streams before comparison. They are informative matched-budget controls, not a claim to cover every possible ranking intervention. The guided selector's additional search is part of the compared configuration; these comparisons are neither equal-operation ablations nor evidence that a particular extra operation is necessary.\n   124\t\n   125\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n   126\t\\subsection{Data Designs and Frozen Evaluation}\n   127\tThe final panels are Arena and NBA seasons 2011--2015. Arena uses the pinned public human-preference snapshot; NBA uses unique games extracted from the pinned FiveThirtyEight historical data. NBA reciprocal records are checked during preparation and represent one game, not independent observations. Elo fields are not estimator inputs. Arena document-normalization hashes define transitive content groups for splitting and resampling; NBA uses unique games. Source names define a fixed lexicographic participant index. The released preparation code and manifests document these choices; hashes identify artifacts but do not substitute for inspecting the implementation.\n   128\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/prepare_data.py#L37-L147]]\n   129\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/panel_manifest.json]]\n   130\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   131\t\n   132\tThe fixed configuration is evaluated under seeds 401, 503, and 607, with every declared panel and budget retained. Development observations are not mixed with these final measurements. The nominal fractions share a common row-cost interpretation, but the resulting absolute costs and graph structures differ substantially (Table~\\ref{tab:panels}). All recorded graphs are connected. NBA has a complete participant-pair graph in each evaluated season; Arena does not. Connectivity alone does not ensure stable score gaps or comparable information per participant.\n   133\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   134\t\n   135\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   136\t\\centering\n   137\t\\caption{Observed final graphs and integer flip costs. Degree counts comparisons incident on a participant, whereas edges count distinct participant pairs. Nominal fractions use all usable rows, including uneditable ties. Equal percentages do not imply equal absolute intervention size or information content.}\n   138\t\\label{tab:panels}\n   139\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n   140\t\\toprule\n   141\tPanel & $p$ & Edges & Min. degree & $k_{.001}$ & $k_{.005}$ & $k_{.01}$\\\\\n   142\t\\midrule\n   143\tArena & 64 & 1265 & 76 & 43 & 216 & 432\\\\\n   144\tNBA 11 & 30 & 435 & 82 & 1 & 6 & 13\\\\\n   145\tNBA 12 & 30 & 435 & 66 & 1 & 5 & 10\\\\\n   146\tNBA 13 & 30 & 435 & 82 & 1 & 6 & 13\\\\\n   147\tNBA 14 & 30 & 435 & 82 & 1 & 6 & 13\\\\\n   148\tNBA 15 & 30 & 435 & 82 & 1 & 6 & 13\\\\\n   149\t\\bottomrule\n   150\t\\end{tabular}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
